er's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Gateway and policy-engine failure behavior is outside this repo; client side _rpc raises on errors.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.5\n\n**Check:** Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Gateway header checks are outside this repo; the client tool list is one process-wide cache shared across tenants.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## E06.6\n\n**Check:** If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.\n\n**How to verify:** 1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that.\n\n**Reviewer's evidence:**  — \"\"\n\n**Reviewer's note:** Gateway OAuth client behavior and discovery URL handling are outside this repo.\n\n(no evidence cited)\n\n## Your answer\n\nWrite this JSON to E06.verdict.json in this folder (or put it in verdicts.json under \"E06\"):\n```json\n{\"verdict\": \"upheld | failed | unclear\",\n \"checks\": [{\"id\": \"E06.1\", \"result\": \"holds | fails | unclear\", \